in the Senegalese capital dakar the  former Palace of Justice has been  converted to house the month-long show  that features contemporary African art  Oh linear Rasheeda Candia a Nigerian  artist living in the u.s. is putting up  his installation from everyday materials  such as cardboard fabric and plastic his  work is an existential reflection of  post-colonial Africa combined with  political and environmental issues it's  a vivid complex creation and materials  that I use are boxes which people throw  away which I cut into pieces and then  also the collections of the slide which  come from different pieces of different  people which I put together to form a  kind of thing so we look like more like  a net but the idea was I'll connect our  connection our link we had to each other  another question which I'm hosting  myself especially with this connection  was to look at what where are we as  Africans where are we now  are we stick crawling are we still  working or I've already start standing  up or we are started working the  Biennale invited 65 artists  what started as a literature festival 20  years ago in Dakar has morphed into  Africa's largest Contemporary Art Fair  lesbian l did the can a la partie bonala  of the car focuses on contemporary art  for both artists in Africa and those in  the Diaspora cool so it's important that  it takes place in the continent landmark  cultural event for the visual arts it's  11 market or el mejor de pontignac or  laser vision the work of Victor at you  comin or another Nigerian artist deals  with the stark changes in his country he  grew up in a society largely influenced  by mythology and religion his  installation is a kind of prayer room  that recreates the world of his  childhood you know places like this they  are all going extinct now you probably  won't find them anymore but I was lucky  enough as a kid to find places like this  where people could go for prayers could  good for definition could good for  prophecy you know so that is what I've  created here and the sculpted paintings  are representation of people that go in  there the kind of clothes they would  wear the kind of colors that is vibrant  as traditions give way to technology  some old ways of life are dying out  while new lifestyles are still being  defined these are the kinds of questions  about transition posed by the dark art  Biennale in Senegal
 

